.TH LINT 8
.SH NAME
lint \- check the tree's prose against ASD-STE100
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tools/ste-lint.py
[
.B \-\-strict
] [
.B \-\-show
] [
.B \-\-full
] [
.BI \-\-rule " name" ...
] [
.I path ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Lint
checks comments and documents against the structural rules of
ASD-STE100, the controlled English the aerospace industry writes
maintenance manuals in.
Odin's own syntax is skipped.
What it reads is prose: the block and line comments in
.B *.odin
files, and the body text of
.B README.md
and
.BR docs/*.md .
With no
.I path
given it walks
.BR README.md ,
.BR docs ,
.B kernel
and
.BR sys .
.PP
The standard has fifty-three rules.
.I Lint
checks the seven a program can decide on its own, with no dictionary
and no judgement call:
.TP 10
.B semicolon
banned outright
.TP
.B length
twenty-five words for descriptive prose, twenty for a string the
kernel prints
.TP
.B perfect
the present and past perfect tense, unless a modal needs the compound
form
.TP
.B passive
a be-verb and a past participle, in a sentence that names an actor the
subject could have been instead
.TP
.B phrasal
a two-word verb whose meaning the parts do not predict
.TP
.B marketing
an adjective that claims a quality instead of showing it
.TP
.B paragraph
more than six sentences on one topic
.PD
.PP
The rest of the standard is lexical: an approved-word dictionary of
about nine hundred words, each pinned to one meaning and one part of
speech.
That dictionary is not redistributable and is not checked here.
.SS Modes
Without
.BR \-\-strict ,
.I lint
checks documents and doc comments only, at the twenty-five word cap.
With
.BR \-\-strict ,
it also reads every string literal passed to a call that fails, panics,
or reports a check
.RB ( check ,
.BR vcheck ,
.BR scheck " and its family, and"
.BR put_str ),
at the twenty-word cap that applies to something the kernel actually
prints.
.PP
.B \-\-show
prints every finding, grouped by file, with the line, the rule and up
to seventy characters of the sentence.
.B \-\-full
prints the whole sentence instead of the first seventy characters.
.B \-\-rule
limits the run to the named rules, and may be given more than once.
Without
.BR \-\-show ,
.I lint
prints only the summary: a count per rule and a total.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
tools/ste-lint.py
tools/ste-lint.py --show kernel/sync/sleep.odin
tools/ste-lint.py --rule semicolon docs
tools/ste-lint.py --strict kernel sys
odin run build.odin -file -out:.vectra-build -- lint --show
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B README.md
.TP
.B docs/*.md
.TP
.B *.odin
under
.B kernel
and
.BR sys ,
the default paths
.SH SOURCE
.B tools/ste-lint.py
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR build (8)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Lint
exits with the count of findings, zero when there are none, so it
works as a build gate.
The tree is kept at zero findings.
A finding is a regression, and the fix is the sentence, not the
checker.
.SH BUGS
The lexical rules are not checked, for want of a redistributable copy
of the approved-word dictionary.
An
.B \-ing
word used as a verb, which the standard also forbids, is not caught.
Telling a verb from a technical noun needs a part-of-speech tagger
this program does not have.
